{"subjectId":"c","id":"error-handling","title":"Error Handling: errno & Return Codes","group":"Files, Preprocessor & Program Organisation","order":52,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">C has no exceptions. Functions report failure through their <strong>return value</strong> (<code>NULL</code>, <code>-1</code>, <code>EOF</code>), and the library adds detail in the global <code>errno</code>. Checking every result is the programmer’s job.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list\"></i>Library conventions</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Function kind</th><th>Failure signal</th><th>Details</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Returns a pointer (<code>fopen</code>, <code>malloc</code>)</td><td><code>NULL</code></td><td><code>errno</code> set (by POSIX)</td></tr><tr><td>Returns a count (<code>fread</code>, <code>scanf</code>)</td><td>Fewer items than requested / <code>EOF</code></td><td><code>feof</code> / <code>ferror</code></td></tr><tr><td>POSIX system calls (<code>open</code>, <code>read</code>)</td><td><code>-1</code></td><td><code>errno</code></td></tr><tr><td>Conversions (<code>strtol</code>)</td><td>Result + <code>end</code> pointer</td><td><code>errno == ERANGE</code> on overflow</td></tr><tr><td>Math (<code>sqrt(-1)</code>, <code>log(0)</code>)</td><td>NaN / ±HUGE_VAL</td><td><code>errno == EDOM</code> / <code>ERANGE</code> (if <code>math_errhandling</code> says so)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bug\"></i>errno, strerror, perror</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;string.h&gt;\n#include &lt;errno.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    FILE *f = fopen(\"does-not-exist.txt\", \"r\");\n    if (f == NULL) {\n        int saved = errno;                 /* save it: the next call may overwrite it */\n        printf(\"fopen failed: errno=%d (%s)\\n\", saved, strerror(saved));\n        printf(\"is ENOENT? %s\\n\", saved == ENOENT ? \"yes\" : \"no\");\n        perror(\"fopen\");                   /* prints \"fopen: No such file...\" to stderr */\n    }\n\n    errno = 0;                             /* strtol only sets errno on error: reset first */\n    long v = strtol(\"99999999999999999999\", NULL, 10);\n    if (errno == ERANGE) printf(\"overflow, clamped to %ld\\n\", v);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>fopen failed: errno=2 (No such file or directory)\nis ENOENT? yes\noverflow, clamped to 9223372036854775807</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>errno</code> is only meaningful right after a function has <em>reported</em> failure. Successful calls may leave any value in it, so never test <code>errno</code> alone to detect an error.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Designing your own error reporting</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\ntypedef enum { ERR_OK = 0, ERR_EMPTY, ERR_RANGE, ERR_PARSE } Err;\n\nconst char *err_str(Err e) {\n    static const char *msg[] = {\"ok\", \"empty input\", \"out of range\", \"not a number\"};\n    return msg[e];\n}\n\n/* return the status; deliver the result through an out-parameter */\nErr parse_percent(const char *s, int *out) {\n    if (!s || !*s) return ERR_EMPTY;\n    int v = 0;\n    for (; *s; s++) {\n        if (*s &lt; '0' || *s &gt; '9') return ERR_PARSE;\n        v = v * 10 + (*s - '0');\n        if (v &gt; 100) return ERR_RANGE;\n    }\n    *out = v;\n    return ERR_OK;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *inputs[] = {\"42\", \"\", \"250\", \"4x\"};\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++) {\n        int p;\n        Err e = parse_percent(inputs[i], &amp;p);\n        if (e == ERR_OK) printf(\"'%s' -&gt; %d%%\\n\", inputs[i], p);\n        else printf(\"'%s' -&gt; error: %s\\n\", inputs[i], err_str(e));\n    }\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>'42' -&gt; 42%\n'' -&gt; error: empty input\n'250' -&gt; error: out of range\n'4x' -&gt; error: not a number</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-back-up\"></i>Cleanup with goto</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;\n\nint process(const char *path) {\n    int rc = -1;\n    char *buf = NULL;\n    FILE *f = fopen(path, \"w+\");\n    if (!f) goto out;\n\n    buf = malloc(64);\n    if (!buf) goto close_file;\n\n    if (fputs(\"data\\n\", f) == EOF) goto free_buf;\n    rewind(f);\n    if (!fgets(buf, 64, f)) goto free_buf;\n    printf(\"read back: %s\", buf);\n    rc = 0;                                 /* success */\n\nfree_buf:                                   /* release in reverse order of acquisition */\n    free(buf);\nclose_file:\n    fclose(f);\nout:\n    return rc;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(\"rc = %d\\n\", process(\"work.txt\"));\n    printf(\"rc = %d\\n\", process(\"no-such-dir/work.txt\"));\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>read back: data\nrc = 0\nrc = -1</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-power\"></i>Terminating</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Call</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>return n;</code> from <code>main</code></td><td>Same as <code>exit(n)</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>exit(status)</code></td><td>Runs <code>atexit</code> handlers, flushes and closes streams, then ends the process</td></tr><tr><td><code>_Exit(status)</code> / <code>_exit</code></td><td>Ends immediately — no handlers, no flushing</td></tr><tr><td><code>abort()</code></td><td>Raises <code>SIGABRT</code> — abnormal termination, core dump; used by <code>assert</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>quick_exit</code> (C11)</td><td>Runs <code>at_quick_exit</code> handlers only</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you handle errors in C without exceptions?</summary><div><p>Return a status code (or <code>NULL</code>/<code>-1</code>) from every fallible function and pass results through out-parameters; check every return value; propagate errors upward; use <code>errno</code> / <code>strerror</code> for library failures; and structure cleanup with the <code>goto cleanup</code> pattern so each resource is released exactly once on every path. <code>setjmp</code>/<code>longjmp</code> exist for non-local jumps but skip cleanup and are rarely appropriate.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Is errno thread-safe?</summary><div><p>Yes on modern systems: since C11, <code>errno</code> is a macro that expands to a <em>thread-local</em> lvalue (often <code>(*__errno_location())</code>), so each thread has its own copy.</p></div></details></section>"}